# Xbox 360 XDBF Container Specification & Low-Level In-Memory Parser Engine

An architectural, low-level reverse-engineering analysis and modular C++/Qt implementation for inspecting, validating, and extracting non-volatile structured metadata from the proprietary **Xbox Database Format (XDBF)**.

---

## 1. Abstract & System Motivation

The Xbox 360 operating system architecture (Xbox 360 System Software / Xenon kernel) manages user identity, stateful title configuration, localized assets, and cryptographic achievements through discrete container files known as **Game Profile Data (GPD)** packages. At the core of every valid `.gpd` package lies an **XDBF (Xbox Database File)** block.

Historically, reverse-engineering embedded console binaries requires navigating:
- **Heterogeneous Endianness Constraints**: Xenon is a multi-core 64-bit PowerPC architecture running strictly in **Big-Endian** byte order. Parsing on $x86\_64$ hosts requires deterministic endian swapping across multibyte integers.
- **Dynamic Sparse Memory Layouts**: To preserve flash and hard-disk drive bandwidth on original 2005 hardware, XDBF records utilize table-driven pointer indirections, run-length sparse tables, and free-allocation heaps.

This repository provides an algorithmic, statically type-safe reference parser and desktop analysis suite capable of bit-level traversal, structural integrity validation, and payload deserialization.

---

## 2. Binary Layout & Formal Specification

An XDBF container operates as a specialized, single-file flat database. All fields are aligned on 32-bit boundaries and encoded in **Big-Endian (`MSB:BE`)**.

```text
+------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|                          XDBF FILE CONTAINER                           |
+------------------------------------------------------------------------+
| 0x00 - 0x17 : Fixed File Header (Magic, Version, Reserved Pointers)    |
+------------------------------------------------------------------------+
| 0x18 - 0x2F : Primary Allocation & Entry Table Pointers                |
+------------------------------------------------------------------------+
| [Dynamic]   : Entry Specifier Table (Array of XDBF_ENTRY)              |
+------------------------------------------------------------------------+
| [Dynamic]   : Free Chunk Allocation Table                              |
+------------------------------------------------------------------------+
| [Dynamic]   : Raw Payload Arena (String Tables, Assets, Achievements)  |
+------------------------------------------------------------------------+
2.1 Fixed Header Specification (XDBF_HEADER)
The primary header spans 24 bytes (0x18) and enforces format validation via a 4-byte magic signature:

Signature=0x58444246(ASCII: ‘‘XDBF")
C++
#pragma pack(push, 1)
struct XdbfHeader {
    uint32_t magic;               // 0x00: 0x58444246 ("XDBF")
    uint32_t version;             // 0x04: Format revision (typically 0x00000001)
    uint32_t entry_table_offset;  // 0x08: Relative offset to Entry Specifiers
    uint32_t entry_count;         // 0x0C: Total populated entry descriptors
    uint32_t free_table_offset;   // 0x10: Relative offset to Free Space Table
    uint32_t free_table_size;     // 0x14: Allocation pool size
};
#pragma pack(pop)
2.2 Entry Descriptor Record (XDBF_ENTRY)
Each indexed resource within the database is referenced by an 18-byte entry structure mapping namespace identifiers, unique asset 64-bit keys, and memory offsets.

C++
#pragma pack(push, 1)
struct XdbfEntry {
    uint16_t namespace_id;        // 0x00: Namespace category (Title, Settings, Icons)
    uint64_t resource_id;         // 0x02: Unique 64-bit Big-Endian resource hash
    uint32_t payload_offset;      // 0x0A: Absolute file byte-offset to raw payload
    uint32_t payload_length;      // 0x0E: Size of payload block in bytes
};
#pragma pack(pop)
2.3 Namespace Identification Mapping
The XDBF format clusters resources using hierarchical semantic namespaces:

Namespace Identifier	Hex Code	Semantic Role
NAMESPACE_ACHIEVEMENT	0x0001	Achievement definitions, reward states, Gamerscore yields
NAMESPACE_ICON	0x0002	Compressed PNG / DDS texture chunks for badges and title banners
NAMESPACE_SETTING	0x0003	User-defined title configurations, input inversion mappings, and audio state
NAMESPACE_STRING	0x0004	UTF-8 / UTF-16 BE localized text descriptors and string lookup tables
NAMESPACE_METADATA	0x0005	Master title ID, content flags, and system hardware specifications
3. Algorithmic Complexity & Systems Design
3.1 Endian Conversion Primitives
Because 
x86_64 hosts operate on Little-Endian registers, all header reading operations must pass through bit-twiddling intrinsics:

bswap 
32
​
 (x)=((x≪24) & 0xFF000000)∣((x≪8) & 0x00FF0000)∣((x≫8) & 0x0000FF00)∣((x≫24) & 0x000000FF)
In this implementation, hardware-accelerated compiler intrinsics (__builtin_bswap32, _byteswap_ulong) or Qt-native qFromBigEndian<T>() guarantee 
O(1) transformations compiled directly into single bswap assembly instructions.

3.2 Dynamic Search & Spatial Verification
Linear Parse Complexity: 
O(N) with respect to entry descriptors during initial file mapping, loading zero-copy pointers directly from memory-mapped disk buffers.

Lookup Complexity: 
O(logN) by ordering entries into a balanced binary search space keyed on (namespace_id, resource_id).

Auxiliary Space Complexity: 
O(N) resident descriptors maintained in flat cache-friendly continuous vectors (std::vector<XdbfEntry>), maximizing L1/L2 cache locality during batch traversal.

4. Software Architecture & Directory Layout
Plaintext
xbox-xdbf-explorer/
├── cmake/
│   └── CompilerFlags.cmake       # Strict warnings (-Wall, -Wextra, -Wpedantic, -Werror)
├── include/
